# Java/Kotlin + Spring testing

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic testing rules to JUnit 5 +
Spring Boot Test conventions, for both Java and Kotlin. Applies only to
`*.java`/`*.kt`/`*.kts` files.

## Layout and naming

- Tests under `src/test/java` or `src/test/kotlin`, mirroring the package of
  the class under test, named `<Type>Test` (unit) or
  `<Type>IT`/`<Type>IntegrationTest` (integration); match the project's
  convention.
- A plain unit test (construct the class directly, pass mocks/fakes to the
  constructor) for logic that does not need the Spring container;
  `@SpringBootTest` only for genuine integration coverage.

## Test slices

- The narrowest Spring test slice: `@WebMvcTest` for a controller (with
  `MockMvc`), `@DataJpaTest` for a repository, `@SpringBootTest` only when
  the interaction across the whole wired context is under test.
- Testcontainers over an in-memory substitute (H2 standing in for
  Postgres/MySQL) when the project already uses Testcontainers.

## Mocking

- Mock/stub only the collaborators the class under test depends on
  (Mockito `@Mock`/`@InjectMocks`, or MockK for Kotlin); no
  `@MockitoBean`/`@SpringBootTest` just to avoid constructing a plain
  dependency by hand. `@MockitoBean` is the annotation for mocking a bean
  inside a Spring test slice; `@MockBean` is deprecated since Boot 3.4 and
  removed in 4.x, so use it only on a project pinned below 3.4.
- Assert on behavior/interactions relevant to the test (`verify(...)`), not
  incidental implementation details.

## Assertions and determinism

- The project's configured assertion library (AssertJ, JUnit's own, or
  Kotlin's `kotlin.test`); do not add a second.
- Never `Thread.sleep`/`delay` to wait for an async result; use Awaitility
  (`await().atMost(...).until(...)`), a `CompletableFuture`/coroutine join,
  or a `CountDownLatch`.
- A validation test asserts the specific constraint violation/exception type.

## Fixtures and lifecycle

- A builder or test-fixture factory method with sensible defaults,
  overriding only the fields the test cares about.
- `@BeforeEach`/`@AfterEach` for per-test setup/teardown; `@BeforeAll`/
  `@AfterAll` only for expensive, safely-shared setup (a Testcontainers
  instance).

## Coverage expectations

- New behavior gets a new test in the same change; a bug fix gets a
  regression test that fails before the fix and passes after.
- Run the build-tool test task (`./gradlew test` or `mvn test`) for any
  change touching test files, and the integration-test task separately when
  configured, before reporting done.
